  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a comprehensive explanation of why nitrogen is used in plastic welding:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd35 The Core Reason: Preventing Oxidation\n\nNitrogen is used for plastic welding because it creates a stronger, cleaner weld compared to hot air. The key issue comes down to oxygen. Air is composed of 80% nitrogen and 20% oxygen, and the oxygen in the air causes oxidation, burning the plastic during the welding process. This results in smoke, ash, and sparks, which weaken the weld and create contamination.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udee1\ufe0f Nitrogen as a Shielding Gas\n\nNitrogen gas is used when plastic welding because it acts as a shielding gas, protecting the weld area from contaminants in the air. A nitrogen hot air welder uses compressed nitrogen gas to eliminate oxygen from the weld area. The nitrogen acts as a shielding gas and allows for a contaminant-free weld with less smoke, which creates a stronger weld.\n\nThis is similar in principle to how shielding gases are used in metal welding \u2014 nitrogen is used as a welding medium, much like shielding gases used when welding steel or aluminum with a MIG welder, to prevent the plastic from oxidizing during the welding process.\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 The Result: A Cleaner, Stronger Weld\n\nWelding with nitrogen eliminates this issue by flushing out the oxygen from the weld zone. Without oxygen, the plastic melts cleanly without burning or oxidizing. This allows the melted plastics to fuse together with no contamination for a stronger, more durable weld.\n\nThe nitrogen shielding gas prevents oxidation, ensuring the strongest welds possible. This means you get a solid, long-lasting weld that stands up to vibration, cold, and heat.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf21\ufe0f Thermodynamic Benefits\n\nBeyond just preventing oxidation, nitrogen also offers thermal advantages. Nitrogen has thermodynamic properties that make it useful when welding plastics like ABS and polypropylene; its high heat capacity allows for faster cooling of the welded material without causing warping or cracking due to thermal shock.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd25 Hot Air vs. Nitrogen: Key Difference\n\nFor years, technicians relied on hot staplers or air-based plastic welders. And while those methods worked to a point, they had their flaws. The problem lies in the oxygen \u2014 when you weld with regular hot air, the oxygen causes oxidation, which weakens the weld and makes it more likely to crack down the road.\n\nIn summary, nitrogen is used in plastic welding primarily because it is an **inert gas** that displaces oxygen, preventing the burning and oxidation that would otherwise compromise the quality and durability of the weld.",
